####
# ======================================================================
# fledge - Ship a Ruby app to people who don't have Ruby
# ======================================================================
#
# SYNOPSIS
#     fledge build [OPTIONS] [PATH]
#     fledge --help
#     fledge --version
#
# DESCRIPTION
#     Put a Ruby app, and a Ruby to run it, in one folder that runs on
#     a computer with no Ruby installed.
#
#     The Ruby on your computer can't travel. It was built for one
#     machine, with that machine's paths and libraries baked in, so a
#     copy breaks anywhere else. Fledge starts instead from a runtime:
#     a Ruby built to be moved, and a small launcher that runs your
#     app on it. Fledge copies your app into the runtime for the
#     computer it runs on, and makes it what that system expects, with
#     that system's own tools; build on a Mac for macOS, on Windows for
#     Windows, and on Linux for Linux, as a CI runner of each does.
#
#     Build the program you already run, such as exe/my_app in a gem,
#     bin/server in an app with a Gemfile, or a lone main.rb. Its
#     project ships with it, and so do the gems its Gemfile.lock locks,
#     each as built for this platform, which Bundler sets up before the
#     program runs, as bundle exec would. A gem with an extension to
#     compile (published only as source, or from git) is compiled
#     against the runtime's own Ruby, with your compiler.
#
#     An app is console (it runs in a terminal) or windowed (it opens
#     its own windows): a windowed app is an .app bundle on macOS,
#     signed ad hoc, a program Windows starts without a console, and a
#     folder with a desktop entry and icons on Linux. Every app carries
#     the name, version, publisher, and icon fledge.yml gives, where
#     each system shows them, on Windows in the program itself. A
#     windowed app gets an installer beside each archive, too: a disk
#     image for macOS, a setup program for Windows, which makes the
#     Start menu shortcut Windows needs before it shows the app's
#     notifications, and an AppImage for Linux.
#
# COMMANDS
#     build              Copy the program PATH (default: the current
#                        directory) and its project into this
#                        computer's runtime, with the gems it needs,
#                        then archive the result, and for a windowed
#                        app write its installer. Prints the path of
#                        the archive and installer it writes, one per
#                        line.
#                        A folder means the one executable its gemspec
#                        names, or else its main.rb.
#
# OPTIONS
#     -o, --out DIR      Write builds to DIR (default: dist)
#     -h, -?, --help     Show this help
#     -v, --version      Print Fledge's version
#
# FILES
#     ROOT               The program's project: the nearest folder,
#                        from the program's up, with a Gemfile,
#                        gems.rb, a gemspec, or a fledge.yml, but not
#                        above a git repository's top; else the
#                        program's own folder. Everything in it ships,
#                        as app/, except .git, .bundle, vendor/bundle,
#                        vendor/cache, and DIR.
#     ROOT/Gemfile.lock  The gems to ship, without the development and
#                        test groups. It must lock this computer's
#                        platform (bundle lock --add-platform), each
#                        gem's checksum (bundle lock --add-checksums),
#                        and each git gem's commit. Every gem ships
#                        with its own files and notices, whatever its
#                        license, which is yours to comply with. A gem
#                        that does not support the runtime's Ruby, or
#                        whose compiled files need a library another
#                        computer may lack, fails the build. A gem with
#                        an extension to compile is compiled with
#                        bundle config build.NAME's options: Xcode's
#                        command line tools on macOS, a C compiler and
#                        make on Linux, RubyInstaller's MSYS2 UCRT64
#                        tools (ridk install) on Windows, and cargo for
#                        a Rust extension.
#     ROOT/fledge.yml    Optional, a YAML mapping of:
#                        name: the app's name (default: the gemspec's
#                          name, or ROOT's folder name, camelized)
#                        kind: console (default) or windowed
#                        identifier: the app's reverse-DNS name, such as
#                          com.example.MyCoolApp, which macOS and
#                          Windows know it by (needed when windowed)
#                        version: such as "1.2.3" (default: the
#                          gemspec's, or 0.0.0)
#                        icon: a square PNG of at least 1024x1024
#                          pixels, such as icon.png, made into each
#                          system's icons
#                        publisher: who makes it (default: the
#                          gemspec's authors)
#                        copyright: such as "© 2026 Example, Inc."
#                        installers: which of dmg, nsis, and appimage a
#                          windowed app gets, such as [dmg] or [] for
#                          none (default: all three)
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM/ The built app, ready to run where it lands.
#                        PLATFORM is this computer's: arm64-darwin,
#                        x86_64-linux, aarch64-linux, x64-mingw-ucrt.
#                        NAME is the gemspec's name, or else ROOT's
#                        folder name, camelized (my_cool_app is
#                        MyCoolApp), unless fledge.yml names it. NAME
#                        is also the program to run (NAME.exe on
#                        Windows), or, for a windowed app on macOS,
#                        the bundle NAME.app; a windowed Linux app has
#                        IDENTIFIER.desktop and share/icons/ beside
#                        it. It runs app/PROGRAM as ruby, or bundle
#                        exec, would, and exits with its status, or
#                        with 66 when it is missing and 70 when the
#                        rest of the folder is broken. Its notices/
#                        holds the licenses of what Fledge put in it;
#                        each gem keeps its own in its folder. A new
#                        build replaces it.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.zip
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.tar.gz
#                        The same folder, archived for download: a .zip
#                        for Windows, a .tar.gz for macOS and Linux.
#     DIR/NAME-arm64-darwin.dmg
#                        A windowed app's disk image: NAME.app and a
#                        link to Applications, to drag it onto.
#     DIR/NAME-x64-mingw-ucrt-setup.exe
#                        A windowed app's NSIS installer, which installs
#                        for the person who runs it, with no
#                        administrator, into %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs,
#                        makes a Start menu shortcut whose
#                        AppUserModelID is the identifier, offers a
#                        desktop shortcut, and registers an uninstaller.
#                        /S installs silently.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.AppImage
#                        A windowed app's AppImage for Linux, which runs
#                        from its own FUSE mount, or, on a computer with
#                        no FUSE, extracts itself and runs
#                        (APPIMAGE_EXTRACT_AND_RUN=1 asks for that).
#     ~/.cache/fledge/runtimes/
#                        Runtimes downloaded from Fledge's GitHub
#                        Release for this version, each checked
#                        against the release's SHA256SUMS; to compile
#                        gems, each one's build-only companion of Ruby's
#                        headers and libraries; and, for an NSIS
#                        installer, NSIS's makensis for this computer;
#                        none of which ships in an app.
#     ~/.cache/fledge/gems/
#                        Gems downloaded from the lockfile's gem
#                        server, each checked against the lockfile's
#                        checksum. ROOT/vendor/cache and RubyGems' own
#                        caches are looked in first.
#     ~/.cache/fledge/git/
#                        Repositories of the lockfile's git gems, which
#                        ship at the commit it locks.
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#     FLEDGE_RUNTIME_DIR Take runtimes from this folder of
#                        fledge-runtime-VERSION-PLATFORM.tar.gz files
#                        (and their -dev.tar.gz companions, and
#                        fledge-makensis-VERSION-PLATFORM.tar.gz), and
#                        never download them.
#     XDG_CACHE_HOME     Keep downloaded runtimes and gems in
#                        $XDG_CACHE_HOME/fledge instead.
#
# EXIT STATUS
#     0                  Success
#     1                  The build failed (a missing app, a computer
#                        Fledge has no runtime for, a runtime that
#                        would not download, a gem that cannot ship or
#                        would not compile, a full disk)
#     2                  Usage error (an unrecognized option, a
#                        missing value)
#
# EXAMPLES
#     Build the gem in the current directory for this computer:
#     fledge build
#
#     Build a gem's executable, here and on a computer of each other
#     platform, such as a CI runner of each:
#     fledge build exe/my_cool_app
#
#     Build a script from elsewhere, into another folder:
#     fledge build --out /tmp/builds path/to/app/main.rb
#
# ======================================================================
###

require "fledge/cli"
exit Fledge::CLI.new(help_path: __FILE__).call(ARGV)
